  "answer": "I rewrote the two writable files.\n\n**`registry.mjs`** — `resolveFromRegistry(path, records)` now implements the specified policy:\n- Validation: throws `TypeError` unless `path` is a nonempty string starting with `/` (checked before any record work).\n- Single pass over `records`: the **first** matching static record returns immediately (static always beats dynamic, regardless of declaration order); the first matching dynamic record is remembered but not returned yet; the first fallback record is remembered.\n- After the loop: first matching dynamic wins if present, else the first fallback, else `null`.\n- Returned `params` are fresh plain objects (`{}` for static/fallback, a shallow copy of the matched dynamic params), so results are independent of later calls and records are never mutated. Matching is delegated to the existing `matchStatic`/`matchDynamic`/`fallbackResult` helpers, which are case-sensitive and treat trailing slashes as distinct.\n\n**`entry.mjs`** — `dispatch(path)` now delegates straight to `resolveFromRegistry(path, defaultRoutes)`, dropping the `legacyResolve` short-circuit so the production route table is the only source of truth. `legacy.mjs` was left untouched, so `legacyResolve('/old/help')` still returns `{ route: 'legacy-help', params: {} }` for its own consumers.\n\nExpected behavior with the fixture: `/users/new` → `user-new` (static beats the earlier dynamic), `/users/Ada` → `user-detail` with `{ id: 'Ada' }`, `/health` → `health`, `/missing` → `not-found`, and `resolveFromRegistry('/x', [])` → `null`.\n\nNo tests were run — the environment provides only file tools, so nothing was executed. The changes were verified by reading the code paths only.",
